         NetHack 5.0 -- seeded games (a fork)

This is a fork of NetHack 5.0 that adds seeded games, so that several
players can race through the same dungeon. Everything below the next
title is the original NetHack README. The file "Seeding" in the top
directory describes seeded games in full and lists every change, grouped
by area.

Usage
  Release tarballs (from the GitHub releases page, one per tagged
  version) run from wherever they're unpacked: "./nethack" in the
  unpacked directory. Racers must all use the same tarball.
  Linux tarballs are built on Ubuntu 22.04. The supported baseline is
  glibc 2.35 or newer, with ncursesw/tinfo 6 and libuuid 1 at runtime.
  CI runs both x86_64 and aarch64 tarballs on Debian 12 and 13 before
  release.
  This prerelease follows upstream 5.0.1 development with bundled Lua
  5.5. Generator version stays at 1 until the first race release; the
  new Tourist quest and Medusa fix change dungeons from earlier builds.
  Match build and data hash as well as seed and generator version.
  Save and bones files must match the current upstream version and save
  revision.
  Seeded saves must also match the current generator version.
  An incompatible save is kept and the game exits without starting anew.

  A player chooses a seed (a number, or text of at most 63 characters
  without commas; runs of spaces count as one):

      OPTIONS=seed:7394023815          (in .nethackrc)
      NETHACKOPTIONS=seed:12345 nethack

  Or a server sets one for every new game in sysconf, which should be a
  long random value:

      SEED=1556329103471883006

  That overrides the players' own and is shown only as
  "seed hidden#<digest>", so racers can tell they share a seed without
  learning it; nothing the game shows can be traced back to it, but a
  short or guessable one can be found by trying guesses. A SEED that
  isn't a valid seed stops new games from starting. The seed is kept in
  the save file. By default, an unseeded game behaves like NetHack 5.0,
  apart from the fixes listed in Seeding A1 (a NETHACKDIR too long to use
  stops the game, for one). Its save files are interchangeable with the
  matching NetHack 5.0 build's, even with managed sessions enabled.

Managed terminal sessions (Unix)
  A trusted launcher can use "nethack --managed-session" to refuse quit
  and save-and-exit commands, including interrupt-to-quit. Refusals take
  no turn; death, escape, ascension and hangup saving still work. This is
  independent of seeds and the live feed. Replay takes the flag from the
  record; a newly launched process needs it passed again on restore.
  A live VM fork inherits it. Keep SHELLERS, WIZARDS and EXPLORERS empty
  in the deployment's trusted sysconf, and keep the PTY master with the
  supervisor. Closing it hangs up and saves the game (Seeding B13).

What's the same for everyone with the same seed
   -  the character (the seed picks role, race, gender and alignment),
      starting inventory, attributes and pet, and object identities
   -  the dungeon, and every level's map, traps, objects and monsters,
      whatever the player did before getting there, in whatever order
      levels are visited, at whatever experience level
   -  on each level, the chance of a wandering monster on a given turn,
      and its species

What still differs
   -  combat, monster movement and anything else decided during play,
      and where wandering monsters appear and which ones a racer meets
   -  the effects of a player's own choices, limited to the monsters
      and objects concerned: genocided species and uniques already
      made (killed or not) are missing, artifacts already made aren't
      made again, aggravate monster or carrying the Amulet makes
      monsters tougher, and some gear changes which monsters are
      peaceful
   -  a few things that depend on visit order or object ids (the Fort
      Ludios portal's level, some shop prices); "Seeding" has the full
      list

Running a race
   -  Knowing the seed lets a player scout it in a practice game, so
      either announce it when everyone starts, or set SEED on a server;
      a hidden seed needs a race installation of its own, counting only
      each racer's first game ("Seeding" part A3 has the details).
   -  Everyone needs the same build and data files, and must not use
      wizard mode. xlogfile records seed=, seedhidden= (1 for the
      server's seed), seedver= (the seed generator version), build= and
      datahash=; the dumplog's header says the same in sentences, and
      it lists, as #levelhash shows, fingerprints of each level as
      generated, to compare with a reference game.
   -  Only records written by the server verify a game. With RECORDFILE
      set in sysconf, in a build with DUMPLOG (the Linux hints define it,
      the macOS ones don't), the server records every key, random seed
      and signal of each seeded game played with the tty interface, and
      "nethack --replay FILE" plays the game again, on screen or with
      --verify just checking, and says whether it came out the same
      (given the race's seed, for a hidden one).

What changed, in brief
   -  Level generation uses random streams derived from the seed: one
      for each level's layout, and one for each monster and object made
      on it, so nothing a player did can shift anything else. Levels
      are generated for a fixed stand-in hero (whose experience level is
      the depth, so a hero who dives meets tougher monsters than in
      NetHack 5.0), and as if nothing had been genocided (genocided
      monsters are then left out).
   -  Game setup (character, inventory, object identities, dungeon)
      and wandering monsters' species also come from the seed; play
      itself stays random, so it can't be practised.
   -  Everything else that could let a player's history reach a level
      is handled where it happens: the clock (the moon's phase is fixed
      and it's never night, so gremlins never steal intrinsics), bones
      (none), the high score list (never used), monster ids,
      tie-breaking sorts and many special cases.
   -  The seed, fingerprints and build are saved, shown and logged (a
      seeded game's save file is marked with a feature bit of its own,
      so NetHack 5.0 won't load it; unseeded ones are unchanged); new
      option seed, sysconf SEED, commands #levelhash and #wizseedfuzz.
   -  Seeded games can be recorded (every key read, random seed drawn
      and signal acted on) and replayed, to watch or to check them:
      RECORDFILE in sysconf (or NH_RECORD=path for your own games) and
      "nethack --replay FILE".
   -  test/seedfuzz.py remakes every level of thousands of seeds under
      changed histories and checks nothing else changes. test/seedcheck.py
      checks startup and seed retention across saves, and
      test/launchtest.py checks managed launch and shutdown;
      see test/README.md.
   -  "nethack --layouts FILE" writes every level's layout (terrain,
      traps, engravings, stairs, rooms, as generated) for the seed in
      sysconf or on standard input, and --layout-hashes only each
      level's hash. The full dump includes rendering rules and each
      level's context for drawing generated terrain, traps and engravings
      with default symbols and colours, plus the unexplored-cell symbol.
      Regular output files are atomically replaced with mode 0600;
      symlinks are refused.
      A full dump is the whole dungeon: keep it as secret as the seed
      (Seeding B14).
   -  With Unix tty and matching real/effective IDs, NETHACK_FEED_FD
      enables a live JSON feed to an inherited descriptor, including full
      level state; SIGUSR1 requests a keyframe even while waiting for
      input, and a game that ends, is saved or is hung up writes a last
      one of the state it ended in. A keyframe names its level's layout
      by its hash and carries only the terrain that differs from it.
      Menus are sent before input and included in keyframes while open.
      The feed only observes the game and copies the end-of-game dump
      only when a dumplog is configured. A slow collector blocks play
      until it reads the feed. Keep a race's feed away from players until
      it ends (Seeding B12).
      Other window ports disable the feed. test/feedtest.py checks it.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

         NetHack 5.0.1 -- General information


NetHack 5.0 is an enhancement to the dungeon exploration game NetHack,
which is a distant descendent of Rogue and Hack, and a direct descendent
of NetHack 3.6.

NetHack 5.0.1 work-in-progress is not a release of NetHack. It consists
primarily of bug fixes for 5.0.0 for an upcoming 5.0.1 release.

The file doc/fixes5-0-1.txt in the source distribution is being updated with
a list of fixes as they are committed.

Because 5.0.1 work-in-progress is not a release, there are likely to still
be bugs. Please don't treat NetHack-5.0.1 work-in-progress as released code.
Use the most recent NetHack 5.0.0 release if stability is paramount.

We're making the 5.0.1 work-in-progress available ahead of its release so
that you can observe, test out, and contribute to its continuing development.
Constructive suggestions, GitHub pull requests, and bug reports are all
welcome and encouraged.


NetHack 5.0 has made some general architectural improvements to the game or
to its building process. Among them, 5.0 has:

 *  The source code is compliant with the C99 standard.

 *  removes barriers to building NetHack on one platform and operating system,
    for later execution on another (possibly quite different) platform and/or
    operating system. That capability is generally known as "cross-compiling."
    See the file "Cross-compiling" in the top-level folder for more information
    on that.

 *  The build-time "yacc and lex"-based level compiler, the
    "yacc and lex"-based dungeon compiler, and the quest text file processing
    previously done by NetHack's "makedefs" utility, have been replaced with
    Lua text alternatives that are loaded and processed by the game during play.

                        - - - - - - - - - - -

Please read items (1), (2) and (3) BEFORE doing anything with your new code.

1.  Unpack the code in a dedicated new directory.  We will refer to that
    directory as the 'Top' directory.  It makes no difference what you
    call it.

2.  Having unpacked, you should have a file called 'Files' in your Top
    directory.

    This file contains the list of all the files you now SHOULD
    have in each directory.  Please check the files in each directory
    against this list to make sure that you have a complete set.

    This file also contains a list of what files are created during
    the build process.

    The names of the directories listed should not be changed unless you
    are ready to go through the makefiles and the makedefs program and change
    all the directory references in them.

3.  Before you do anything else, please read carefully the file called
    "license" in the 'dat' subdirectory.  It is expected that you comply
    with the terms of that license, and we are very serious about it.

4.  If you are attempting to build NetHack on one platform/processor, to
    produce a game on a different platform/processor it may behoove you to
    read the file "Cross-compiling" in your Top directory.

5.  If everything is in order, you can now turn to trying to get the program
    to compile and run on your particular system.  It is worth mentioning
    that the default configuration is SysV/Sun/Solaris2.x (simply because
    the code was housed on such a system).

    The files sys/*/Install.*, or sys/*/NewInstall.* were written to guide
    you in configuring the program for your operating system.  The files
    win/*/Install.* are available, where necessary, to help you in
    configuring the program for particular windowing environments.
    Reading them, and the man pages, should answer most of your questions.


    At the time of the most recent official release, NetHack 5.0, it had
    been tested to run and/or compile on:

        Intel Pentium or better running Linux, BSDI
            [compiles, runs]
        Intel Pentium or better running Windows 10 or 11
            [compiles, runs]
        Intel-based, or Apple M1, M2, M3, M4, M5 Macs running
            macOS 10.11 (El Capitan) to macOS 26 (Tahoe)
            [compiles, runs]
            (follow the instructions in sys/unix/NewInstall.unx)
        Intel 80386 or greater running MS-DOS with DPMI
            [cross-compiles on Linux, runs on MS-DOS, emulator/dosbox]
            built via djgpp compiler (native or Linux-hosted cross-compiler)
        AmigaOS 3.0 or later
            [cross-compiles on Linux or macOS, runs on Amiga or emulator]
            Running requires Kickstart 39+
            6 MB free RAM minimum (8 MB recommended)
            Hard drive with ~5 MB free space

    There has also been some success runing/comiling on:
        OpenVMS (aka VMS) V8.4 on Alpha and on Integrity/Itanium/IA64
        OpenVMS (ask VMS) V9.2-3 on x86-64

                        - - - - - - - - - - -

If you have problems building the game, or you find bugs in it, we recommend
filing a bug report from our "Contact Us" web page at:
    https://www.nethack.org/common/contact.html
Please include the version information from #version or the command line
option --version in the appropriate field.

A public repository of the latest NetHack code that we've made
available can be obtained via git here:
    https://github.com/NetHack/NetHack
      or
    https://sourceforge.net/p/nethack/NetHack/

When sending correspondence, please observe the following:
o Please be sure to include your machine type, OS, and patchlevel.
o Please avoid sending us binary files (e.g. save files or bones files).
  If you have found a bug and think that your save file would aid in solving
  the problem, send us a description in words of the problem, your machine
  type, your operating system, and the version of NetHack.  Tell us that you
  have a save file, but do not actually send it.
  You may then be contacted by a member of the development team with the
  address of a specific person to send the save file to.
o Though we make an effort to reply to each bug report, it may take some
  time before you receive feedback.  This is especially true during the
  period immediately after a new release, when we get the most bug reports.
o We don't give hints for playing the game.
o Don't bother to ask when the next version will be out or you can expect
  to receive a stock answer.

If you want to submit a patch for the NetHack source code via email directly,
you can direct it to this address:
    nethack-bugs (at) nethack.org

If a feature is not accepted you are free, of course, to post the patches
to the net yourself and let the marketplace decide their worth.

All of this amounts to the following:  If you decide to apply a free-lanced
patch to your NetHack 5.0 code, you are welcome to do so, of course, but we
won't be able to provide support or receive bug reports for it.

In our own patches, we will assume that your code is synchronized with ours.

                  -- Good luck, and happy Hacking --

